  "raw_table_text": "Table 1. Baseline characteristics of patients with and without ICU-level intervention within 24 hours after MET activation.\nVariable\nOverall (n = 533)\nICU-level intervention (n = 303)\nNo ICU-level intervention (n = 230)\nSMD\n14-day mortality, n (%)\n147 (27.6)\n120 (39.6)\n27 (11.7)\n0.673\nMET-to-event time, days\n11 [5–26]\n14 [6–30]\n9 [4–22]\n0.181\nAge, years\n73 [62–80]\n72 [62–79]\n74 [63–81]\n0.020\nMale, n (%)\n341 (64.0)\n207 (68.3)\n134 (58.3)\n0.210\nEmergency admission, n (%)\n302 (56.7)\n172 (56.8)\n130 (56.5)\n0.005\nBody mass index, kg/m²\n21.2 [18.4–24.5]\n21.3 [18.8–24.8]\n21.0 [18.3–24.3]\n0.167\nComorbidities\nCancer, n (%)\n259 (48.6)\n157 (51.8)\n102 (44.3)\n0.150\nCardiac disease, n (%)\n136 (25.5)\n82 (27.1)\n54 (23.5)\n0.083\nNeurologic disease, n (%)\n122 (22.9)\n68 (22.4)\n54 (23.5)\n0.025\nTrauma, n (%)\n46 (8.6)\n29 (9.6)\n17 (7.4)\n0.078\nLaboratory data\nAlbumin, g/dL\n2.6 [2.0–3.1]\n2.4 [1.9–2.9]\n2.9 [2.4–3.3]\n0.646\nBlood urea nitrogen, mg/dL\n22.2 [14.5–38.8]\n27.5 [17.1–44.6]\n18.2 [12.4–29.0]\n0.515\nC-reactive protein, mg/dL\n4.34 [0.92–11.78]\n6.39 [1.54–14.86]\n2.54 [0.52–7.56]\n0.512\nCreatinine, mg/dL\n1.01 [0.68–1.69]\n1.17 [0.72–2.08]\n0.85 [0.65–1.30]\n0.178\nHemoglobin, g/dL\n9.8 [8.3–11.6]\n9.5 [8.2–11.3]\n10.2 [8.7–11.7]\n0.216\nPotassium, mmol/L\n4.10 [3.60–4.70]\n4.30 [3.70–5.00]\n4.00 [3.50–4.40]\n0.443\nSodium, mmol/L\n138 [135–141]\n138 [134–142]\n138 [135–141]\n0.108\nTotal bilirubin, mg/dL\n0.58 [0.36–0.95]\n0.64 [0.38–1.06]\n0.51 [0.34–0.82]\n0.171\nWhite blood cells, × 10³/µL\n9.7 [5.8–14.5]\n11.1 [6.0–16.8]\n8.5 [5.8–12.7]\n0.258\nVital signs\nBody temperature, °C\n36.7 [36.4–37.0]\n36.7 [36.5–37.0]\n36.7 [36.4–37.1]\n0.014\nRespiratory rate,/min\n17 [15 –18]\n17 [15 –18]\n16 [15 –18]\n0.125\nHeart rate,/min\n84 [73–99]\n86 [74–102]\n82 [71–96]\n0.220\nSystolic blood pressure, mmHg\n126 [111–141]\n126 [110–142]\n126 [112–140]\n0.033\nFootnote Values are presented as median [interquartile range] for continuous variables and number (percentage) for categorical variables.Standardized mean differences (SMDs) were calculated to assess the magnitude of differences between the ICU-level intervention and no ICU-level intervention groups. Abbreviations MET, medical emergency team; SMD, standardized mean difference.\nThe distribution of clinical outcomes is presented in\nS1 Table\n. Fourteen-day mortality occurred in 147 patients (27.6%). Within 24 hours after MET activation, 303 patients (56.8%) required ICU-level interventions. Vasopressor or inotropic infusion was administered to 262 patients (49.2%), mechanical ventilation was initiated in 206 patients (38.6%), and renal replacement therapy or PMX was initiated in 47 patients (8.8%).\nMultivariable Logistic Regression Analysis\nIn the multivariable logistic regression analysis, lower serum albumin levels and higher levels of BUN, CRP, potassium, and white blood cell count were significantly associated with ICU-level intervention within 24 hours after MET activation (\nTable 2\n). Serum albumin showed an inverse association with ICU-level intervention (OR 0.527, 95% CI 0.392–0.707, p < 0.001). In contrast, higher BUN (OR 1.018, 95% CI 1.006–1.029, p = 0.002), CRP (OR 1.029, 95% CI 1.003–1.056, p = 0.030), potassium (OR 1.296, 95% CI 1.054–1.594, p = 0.014), and white blood cell count (OR 1.030, 95% CI 1.004–1.057, p = 0.026) were associated with increased odds of ICU-level intervention. Age, sex, respiratory rate, heart rate, systolic blood pressure, and cancer status were not significantly associated with ICU-level intervention.\n"
}